ds. Let me consider my options:\n\n1. **Fold (-1)**: This would be wasteful with such a strong starting hand. Pocket pairs, especially medium-high pairs like 10s, have good equity against most hands.\n\n2. **Check (0)**: My opponent just called the big blind (action 0). Checking here would be passive and miss an opportunity to build the pot with a premium hand. However, it does allow me to see the flop cheaply.\n\n3. **Raise (200+)**: This is the most aggressive option. A standard raise of 200 (2x the big blind of 100) would:\n   - Build the pot when I likely have the best hand pre-flop\n   - Get information about my opponent's hand strength\n   - Potentially win the pot immediately if they fold\n   - Take control of the hand\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: This is too aggressive for this early stage with 19,900 chips. It risks my entire stack unnecessarily when I can extract more value through strategic play.\n\nGiven that:\n- Pocket 10s are a strong hand that plays well against most ranges\n- I'm currently down in cumulative chips and need to be competitive\n- My opponent just called, showing no particular strength\n- This is only hand 3 of 20, so I have time to recover but should capitalize on good hands\n\nReason:\nI should raise to 200 chips. This accomplishes multiple goals: it builds the pot with a premium hand, allows me to gauge my opponent's strength based on their response, and gives me control of the betting. Pocket 10s are strong enough to play aggressively pre-flop but not so strong that I need to go all-in. A standard raise maintains flexibility for post-flop play while maximizing value.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
