 strongest non-pair hands in Texas Hold'em. It has:\n- High card potential (both face cards)\n- Straight potential (connecting cards)\n- Flush potential (both hearts)\n- Good equity against most hands\n\n**Position & Action:**\n- Opponent checked (action: 0), showing no aggression\n- I'm in position to act after seeing opponent's check\n- Stack: 19,900 chips (starting stack minus small blind of 100)\n\n**Promising Moves:**\n\n1. **Check (0)**: Take a free flop and see community cards without investing more. However, this wastes the value of a strong hand and gives opponent a free chance to improve.\n\n2. **Raise 200**: A small raise (2x big blind) to build the pot with a strong hand. This extracts value and puts pressure on weaker hands while not over-committing.\n\n3. **Raise 400-600**: A standard 3-4x big blind raise. More aggressive, builds a bigger pot with our strong holding.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Far too aggressive pre-flop with K-Q, even suited. Would only be called by better hands typically.\n\n5. **Fold (-1)**: Terrible option with such a strong starting hand.\n\n**Reason:**\n\nI should raise to 400 chips. This is a standard 4x big blind pre-flop raise that:\n- Builds the pot with a premium hand\n- Puts pressure on marginal hands\n- Doesn't over-commit if opponent has a monster (AA, KK)\n- Maintains proper sizing for future streets\n- Takes initiative in the hand\n\nK♥Q♥ is strong enough to play aggressively pre-flop, especially when opponent showed weakness by checking. A 400 chip raise is optimal - not too small to give proper odds, not too large to over-commit.\n\n**Answer: 400**"}
